{
    "suite": "human-plus-tool-admission",
    "schema_version": 1,
    "corpus_version": "0.1.0",
    "generated_by": "prism-parity:tools/generate-tool-admission.php for php; the ports' own runners for ts and py. Every row is execution output from the real TrustPolicy and ToolDefinition -- a hand-authored verdict would assert the author's model of a regex rather than the regex.",
    "scope": "Whether a surface-declared tool is ADMITTED, and the digest its pin is compared against. The relay, the attachment store and the result guard are not covered here.",
    "encoding": "`input_schema_json` is the schema as RAW JSON TEXT, parsed by each language itself. It is NOT a decoded object, and that is load-bearing: an authored `{}` survives a PHP round-trip as `[]` and an authored `1.0` as `1`, so a decoded corpus would have silently replaced the two inputs whose rows exist to test them -- and all three languages would then have agreed, on the wrong input. Same reason json-container-identity carries its 2^53 values as raw strings. Text is also what the wire actually carries: a surface sends JSON, each language parses it natively, and the digests have to match anyway.",
    "recorded": {
        "digest": "ToolDefinition::digest() -- the pin material.",
        "declared": "Whether assertDeclared() passes, i.e. whether discovery may be sent at all.",
        "allows": "The public allows(name) predicate. False for a human-only name AND for a name outside the allowlist.",
        "admitted": "Whether assertAllows(tool) passes. Reading it together with `allows` separates the rules without parsing prose: allows=false means the NAME was refused, while allows=true with admitted=false means the PIN was.",
        "message": "Recorded but DELIBERATELY NOT COMPARED. No language exposes a machine-readable refusal code -- all three carry only an English sentence -- so wording is the only discriminator, and comparing it would make every refusal row disagree on prose while hiding whether the DECISION agreed. The asymmetry is itself a finding; see the manifest."
    },
